          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.086,
            0.848,
            0.125
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Let us consider another expression, 5k – 2, and see the values it takes for different values of k."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.13,
            0.595,
            0.186
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Numbers that leave a remainder of 3 when divided by 5 can also be seen as 2 less than multiples of 5; 5k – 2, where k ≥ 1."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.609,
            0.129,
            0.865,
            0.182
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "table_body",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.609,
                0.129,
                0.865,
                0.182
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_10_table_3.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tr><td>k=</td><td>|1 2 3 4 5</td></tr><tr><td>5k-2=|</td><td>3 8 13 18 23</td></tr></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.193,
            0.604,
            0.234
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Are there other expressions that generate numbers that are 3 more than a multiple of 5?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.247,
            0.293,
            0.269
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Figure it Out"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.272,
            0.847,
            0.293
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. The sum of four consecutive numbers is 34. What are these numbers?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.302,
            0.848,
            0.341
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Suppose p is the greatest of five consecutive numbers. Describe the other four numbers in terms of p."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.35,
            0.848,
            0.426
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. For each statement below, determine whether it is always true, sometimes true, or never true. Explain your answer. Mention examples and non-examples as appropriate. Justify your claim using algebra."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.203,
            0.429,
            0.719,
            0.45
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) The sum of two even numbers is a multiple of 3."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.453,
            0.846,
            0.492
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) If a number is not divisible by 18, then it is also not divisible by 9."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.193,
            0.495,
            0.847,
            0.533
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iii) If two numbers are not divisible by 6, then their sum is not divisible by 6."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.194,
            0.536,
            0.832,
            0.557
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iv) The sum of a multiple of 6 and a multiple of 9 is a multiple of 3."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.198,
            0.56,
            0.832,
            0.581
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(v) The sum of a multiple of 6 and a multiple of 3 is a multiple of 9."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.589,
            0.848,
            0.647
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Find a few numbers that leave a remainder of 2 when divided by 3 and a remainder of 2 when divided by 4. Write an algebraic expression to describe all such numbers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.661,
            0.535,
            0.681
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. “I hold some pebbles, not too many,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.18,
            0.683,
            0.648,
            0.847
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When I group them in 3’s, one stays with me. Try pairing them up — it simply won’t do, A stubborn odd pebble remains in my view. Group them by 5, yet one’s still around, But grouping by seven, perfection is found. More than one hundred would be far too bold, Can you tell me the number of pebbles I hold?”"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.673,
            0.659,
            0.928,
            0.813
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 17,
              "bbox": [
                0.673,
                0.659,
                0.928,
                0.813
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_10_image_17.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.855,
            0.848,
            0.913
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. Tathagat has written several numbers that leave a remainder of 2 when divided by 6. He claims, “If you add any three such numbers, the sum will always be a multiple of 6.” Is Tathagat’s claim true?"
            }
          ]
        },
